  "summary": "Synthesis fidelity and regional morphometry now determine whether imaging-derived features support downstream inference.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a pattern of deriving quantitative inference from imaging through explicit modeling rather than direct measurement, though the methods differ. NeCS-Flow synthesizes gadolinium-free post-contrast glioma T1w MRI using necrotic scaling and rectified-flow refinement, producing contrast-enhanced appearance without gadolinium. A second glioma study fits a 1D reaction-diffusion model to radial tumor density from single time-point MRI segmentations to estimate the migration-to-proliferation ratio. The hematoma study applies transport-based morphometry to admission non-contrast CT, using regional linear models to predict expansion with validation AUROC 0.787. What is new across these preprints is the attempt to extract biologically or clinically interpretable quantities, such as proliferation ratios or regional expansion risk, from single-timepoint or non-contrast acquisitions. Whether this direction generalizes beyond the reported validation remains unestablished by the items.",
